export const EXERCISE_DESCRIPTIONS_ES_EN: Record<string, string> = {
  "Press inclinado máquina": "Sit on the machine and place your feet on the floor.\nGrip the handles in a pronated position (palms facing down) and set your hands slightly wider than shoulder width.\nAs you inhale, lower the handles to mid-chest height and then press them back up.\nKeep your elbows perpendicular to your body and don't lock them out at the top.\nExhale as you finish pressing up.",
  "Press declinado en máquina": "Sit on the machine and place your feet on the floor.\nGrip the handles in a pronated position (palms facing down) and set your hands slightly wider than shoulder width.\nAs you inhale, lower the handles to mid-chest height and then press them back up.\nKeep your elbows perpendicular to your body and don't lock them out at the top.\nExhale as you finish pressing up.",
  "Press plano en máquina": "Sit on the machine and place your feet on the floor.\nGrip the handles in a pronated position (palms facing down) and set your hands slightly wider than shoulder width.\nAs you inhale, lower the handles to mid-chest height and then press them back up.\nKeep your elbows perpendicular to your body and don't lock them out at the top.\nExhale as you finish pressing up.",
  "Sentadilla hack": "To perform this exercise, position yourself on the machine with your shoulders under the pads.\nIt's safer for your back than a classic squat.\nThe quadriceps are the main muscles worked in this exercise, while the hamstrings play a secondary role and are engaged to a lesser degree than the other muscles mentioned.",
  "Pull over máquina": "This exercise follows the same execution as the base exercise, but using a specific machine.\nThis allows for greater focus and isolation on the specific muscles being worked independently.",
  "Prensa vertical": "Be careful when using this machine to avoid arching your back and lifting your hips off the pad.\nWhen using this machine, the effort required is much greater compared to the incline press, even with the same weight.",
  "Curl femoral de pie": "In this exercise, stand up and grip the apparatus with your torso slightly bent to avoid tension in the rectus femoris and make the movement easier.\nPlace one foot in front of the roller pad and make sure your knee is aligned with the machine's axis.",
  "Hip thrust máquina": "In this exercise, lie back on the machine.\nSecure your hips with the strap or roller pad and perform a hip thrust, pushing the roller upward.\nYour foot position should be such that at peak contraction your knees form a 90-degree angle.",
  "Remo en punta": "The stance for this exercise is standing with a straight back, hips flexed around 45 degrees, and knees slightly bent.\nThen pull with your elbows out and bring the bar toward your abdomen.\nDuring this movement, the whole body can be slightly involved.\nBecause of the position, it's important to inhale just before lowering the weight, hold your breath through the pull, and exhale during the final part of the lift without releasing all your air.",
  "Remo en T": "To start the exercise, stand with the T-bar between your legs, rest your chest on the pad, and grip the handles — that's your starting position.\nInhale and bend your elbows as you pull the bar toward your chest, exhale at the end of the movement, then slowly return to the start as you extend your arms.",
  "Remo máquina": "To perform this exercise, sit with your chest and abdomen supported.\nGrip the handle and pull it toward your stomach while pushing your chest out to achieve a good lat contraction.\nA key advantage of this exercise is that it avoids strain on the lower back since the torso is supported.\nThe downside is that it can restrict breathing due to the compression on the abdomen and chest.",
  "Remo alto en máquina": "12 Chest-supported incline row",
  "Aperturas en máquina contractora": "For this exercise, sit with your back supported and grip the handles at mid-chest height.\nThen push with your elbows to mimic a fly motion.\nThe advantage of this exercise over a standard fly is that muscle tension stays constant throughout the range of motion.",
  "Extensión de Cuádriceps": "To perform this exercise, sit on the machine with the top of your ankles under the padded rollers.\nThe back of your knees should be at the edge of the seat, aligned with the machine's axis.\nThen raise the weight until your legs are nearly fully extended, and lower it slowly in a controlled eccentric contraction.",
  "Prensa 45": "To perform this exercise, lie back on the sled press with your back and hips supported.\nYour feet should be placed on the platform slightly wider than hip-width, with your toes turned out slightly.\nThen lower under control until your thighs come close to your torso without lifting your hips off the backrest.\nFinally, press back up in a controlled but explosive manner until you're nearly at full extension.",
  "Prensa en máquina": "To perform this exercise, sit on the machine with your back and hips supported.\nYour feet should be placed on the platform slightly wider than hip-width, with your toes turned out slightly.\nThen lower under control until your thighs come close to your torso without lifting your hips off the backrest.\nFinally, press back up in a controlled but explosive manner until you're nearly at full extension.",
  "Patada de glúteo máquina": "Get into position on the machine, with your torso slightly bent forward resting on the pad and your hands gripping the supports.\nPlace your foot on the footplate and push it back.\nThe movement is controlled, rising to just past horizontal.\nFocus on squeezing the glute of the working leg.",
  "Curl femoral sentado": "Sit on the machine, gripping and pushing against the thigh pads to stay pressed into the seat and stay stable.\nWith your feet in plantar flexion, position the rollers near your heels with your knees aligned with the machine's axis.",
  "Curl femoral tumbado": "Lie face down on the machine with your chest and stomach against the pad, and grip the handles.\nWith your feet in plantar flexion, position the rollers near your heels with your knees off the bench, aligned with the machine's axis.\nFrom near full extension, curl explosively as far as possible, then lower under control.",
  "Jalón al pecho agarre prono": "To perform this exercise, sit on the machine with your legs secured under the pads and your back straight.\nGrip the bar in a pronated grip (knuckles facing back) slightly wider than shoulder width.\nThen pull the bar down to the upper chest while arching your back slightly, pushing your chest out and leaning your body back a little.",
  "Jalón al pecho agarre neutro": "To perform this exercise, sit on the machine with your legs secured under the pads and your back straight.\nGrip the bar with a neutral grip (palms facing each other), then pull the bar down to the upper chest while arching your back slightly, pushing your chest out and leaning back a little.",
  "Jalón al pecho agarre supino": "To perform this exercise, sit on the machine with your legs secured under the pads and your back straight.\nGrip the bar in a supinated grip (palms facing you), then pull the bar down to the upper chest while arching your back slightly, pushing your chest out and leaning back a little.",
  "Jalón agarre cerrado": "To perform this exercise, sit on the machine with your legs secured under the pads and your back straight.\nGrip the close/narrow handle, then pull it down to the upper chest while arching your back slightly, pushing your chest out and leaning back a little.",
  "Jalón unilateral": "Sitting at the lat pulldown machine, grip the handle with one hand and pull it toward your side, driving your elbow in that direction.\nYour other hand can rest on your hip or hold onto the leg pads.",
  "Jalón trasnuca": "To perform this exercise, sit on the machine with your legs secured under the pads and your back straight.\nGrip the bar in a pronated grip (palms facing down), slightly wider than shoulder width.\nThen bring your torso more upright and lean your head forward to lower the bar behind your neck.",
  "Pull down máquina": "To perform this exercise, sit on the machine with your legs secured under the pads and your back straight.\nPlace your hands on the indicated handles, then pull down to the upper chest while arching your back slightly, pushing your chest out and leaning back a little.",
  "Remo gironda": "Sit at the V-bar cable row, with your knees slightly bent and firmly planted on the footplates, and grip the handle with a neutral grip.\nThen extend your arms almost fully and pull the handle toward your stomach while pushing your chest out to achieve a proper contraction.\nAccompany the movement with a slight lean from the lower back.",
  "Pájaros en máquina contractora": "Sitting on the machine, press your chest against the pad to keep it still.\nGrip the handles in front of you and move your elbows away from your torso so your arms are horizontal to the floor.\nKeep your elbows slightly bent and, without changing that angle, open your arms outward.",
  "Elevaciones laterales en máquina": "Sitting on the machine, rest your back or chest (depending on the machine) to keep your torso still.\nThen place your elbows on the corresponding pads, and keeping them bent, start the movement by pushing with your elbows.",
  "Curl predicador en máquina": "Sit on the machine and rest your triceps on the pad, positioning your armpits at the edge and aligning your elbows with the machine's axis.\nGrip the handles and curl the weight up forcefully.\nThen lower the weight until it's a few degrees short of full extension, before returning to the starting position.",
  "Curl de bíceps en máquina": "Sit on the machine and rest your elbows on the pad, aligned with the machine's axis.\nGrip the handles and curl the weight up forcefully.\nThen lower the weight until it's a few degrees short of full extension, before returning to the starting position.",
  "Crunch abdominal en máquina": "Sit on the machine, secure your legs under the pads, and grip the handles above you.\nThen crunch your torso forward by pulling on the handles.",
  "Aductores en máquina": "Sit on the adductor machine and place the pads against the inside of your knees.\nOpen your legs as far as you comfortably can, then close them back together.",
  "Abductores en máquina": "Sit on the machine and place the pads against the outside of your knees.\nOpen your legs as far as you comfortably can, then close them back together.",
  "Cruce poleas ascendente": "Position yourself standing between the pulleys, with your knees slightly bent.\nIt's a good idea to stagger one leg forward.\nStart with your arms extended out to the sides and your elbows slightly bent.\nThen bring your arms together as if giving a hug, moving forward and upward.",
  "Cruce poleas descendente": "Position yourself standing between the pulleys, with your knees slightly bent.\nIt's a good idea to stagger one leg forward and lean your torso slightly.\nStart with your arms extended out to the sides and your elbows slightly bent.\nThen bring your arms together as if giving a hug, moving forward and downward, keeping your elbow bend constant throughout the movement.",
  "Patada de glúteo polea": "To perform this exercise, kneel on a bench and rest your hands on it.\nAttach the cable to your ankle and lift your knee, keeping it bent.\nThen kick the leg upward and back.\nPerform the movement under control, raising the leg to just past horizontal without arching your back too much.",
  "Pull through": "To perform this exercise, stand upright with a slightly wider stance than normal, gripping the cable handle between your legs.\nPush your hips back, keeping your spine neutral throughout.\nYour head should follow the same pattern as your hips.",
  "Glúteo medio en polea": "To perform this exercise, stand sideways next to the low pulley, hold on for support, and keep enough distance to allow a full range of motion.\nThen pull the cable attached to the ankle of the leg farthest from the pulley and lift that leg outward.\nTry to perform a pure abduction without flexing the hip much, staying sideways to avoid turning it into a hip flexion.",
  "Pull over en polea": "To perform this exercise, stand facing the high pulley with your torso slightly bent forward.\nGrip the chosen attachment about shoulder-width apart and keep your elbows nearly locked in a fixed position.\nThen lower the attachment without bending your elbows, pulling with your lats until it reaches your waist.\nAt the bottom, push your chest out for a stronger contraction.",
  "Jalón polea unilateral banco inclinado": "To perform this exercise, sit with your chest against an incline bench and grip the high pulley handle, pull it forcefully with your elbow toward your side, then return slowly to the start.\nTry not to let your shoulder drop and let your lat drive the movement.",
  "Remo horizontal banco polea unilateral": "To perform this exercise, sit on a slightly inclined bench, rest one hand on the backrest, and with the other grip the pulley handle at chest height, pull it forcefully with your elbow toward your waist, then return slowly to the start.\nTry not to let your shoulder drop and let your lat drive the movement.",
  "Elevaciones laterales en polea": "To perform this exercise, first stand sideways facing the low pulley with your legs slightly bent and apart for good posture.\nGrip the handle with your elbow slightly bent at your side and bring it in front of your body.\nKeep your elbow and torso still while you raise your arm in abduction.\nMake sure your elbow reaches the same height as your hand and shoulder as you lift.",
  "Elevaciones frontales en polea": "In this exercise, stand with your back to the low pulley, feet slightly apart and torso braced.\nGrip the handle with a pronated grip.\nKeep your elbows bent and raise your arms forward, bringing them up to head height without changing your elbow bend.",
  "Facepull": "In this movement, start with your arms extended, keeping your elbows close to your torso and staying upright.\nFrom this position, pull the cable toward your neck or face, combining horizontal shoulder extension, abduction, and external rotation.",
  "Cruce posterior en poleas": "Standing with your legs slightly apart and bent, torso braced, grip the handles with a neutral grip, elbows pointing back.\nPull back as if pushing with your elbows, keeping the elbow bend unchanged until reaching torso height.\nYour elbows should stay far enough from your torso to avoid over-involving the lats.",
  "Extensión de Tríceps con cuerda en polea alta": "In this exercise, stand facing the high pulley and grip the rope with your hands closer together than shoulder-width.\nKeep your feet slightly apart and parallel, or stagger one forward, while bracing your torso.\nLower the rope in extension without moving your elbows, keeping them tucked close to your torso.\nWhen you're nearly at full extension, raise the rope back up under control.",
  "Extensión de tríceps overhead con cuerda": "In this exercise, stand with your back to the high or low pulley, one foot underneath it and the other stepped well forward, carrying your weight.\nGripping the rope overhead with your elbows close to your head, extend your arms forward without changing your elbow position.\nKeep your torso slightly bent forward and your abs braced in a strong isometric contraction.",
  "Extensión overhead unilateral polea": "In this exercise, stand upright with your back to the low pulley and grip the rope with one hand.\nThen extend your arm upward, keeping it vertical, without changing your elbow position.",
  "Patada de tríceps unilateral en polea": "To perform this exercise, position yourself with your hip flexed, either braced against the pulley tower or resting on a flat bench.\nPlace the hand and knee of one side on the bench while your opposite hand grips the pulley handle in a neutral position.\nThroughout the exercise, your torso should stay horizontal and aligned at all times.\nStart the movement with your elbow bent, then extend your triceps until you're nearly at full extension.\nIt's important to keep your elbow close to your torso and not let it drift out of position throughout the exercise.",
  "Curl de bíceps con barra en polea": "This exercise involves standing in front of a low pulley, with your legs slightly apart for balance and your torso braced.\nHold the bar in a supinated grip (palms facing up), slightly wider than shoulder-width, in front of your torso.\nThen curl the bar upward under control until you reach maximum flexion.\nIt's important to keep the movement controlled at all times and avoid any jerky or explosive motions.",
  "Curl martillo polea": "This exercise involves standing in front of a low pulley, with your legs slightly apart for balance and your torso braced.\nUsing a rope attachment, your hands are held in a neutral position.\nThen curl upward under control until you reach maximum flexion.\nIt's important to keep the movement controlled at all times and avoid any jerky or explosive motions.",
  "Curl en polea alta": "This exercise requires standing sideways at a distance from the high pulley, with your torso braced.\nThen grip the handle in a supinated position (palms facing up) and extend your arms, without fully locking them out.\nNext, pull toward your neck without changing your elbows' position in space.\nIt's important to keep your torso position steady and avoid jerky movements throughout the exercise.",
  "Curl bíceps tumbado polea": "Lying on the rowing pulley bench, or on the floor in front of a low pulley with the cable running between your legs, hold the bar in front of your torso with a supinated grip (palms facing up).\nCurl it up under control to maximum flexion.",
  "Curl bayesian": "This exercise requires standing with your back to the low pulley, one foot staggered forward for stability, torso braced.\nWith your elbow positioned slightly behind your torso, grip the handle in a supinated position (palms facing up).\nThen curl your elbow without changing its position in space.\nIt's important to keep your torso position steady and avoid jerky movements throughout the exercise.",
  "Crunch en polea": "To perform this exercise, kneel in front of the high pulley and grip the rope or bar behind your neck, hands facing back in a supinated position.\nMake sure to lock your arms so the bar doesn't move from its starting position.\nThen, starting from a fully extended spine, contract your abs and bring your ribs toward your pelvis.\nThe right feeling should be like you're \"curling\" rather than \"bending at the hips.\"\nRemember to keep your hips still throughout the exercise.",
  "Press pallof": "To perform this exercise, stand sideways away from the pulley, feet shoulder-width apart in a stable stance, and hold the handle close to your chest.\nThe pulley will try to rotate your body.\nWhat you need to do is brace, breathe, and press the handle away from your body while resisting the rotation with your core.",
  "Press banca en multipower": "Lying on a flat bench in the Smith machine, with your head, back, and feet firmly supported, grip the bar symmetrically slightly wider than shoulder-width, directly above your eyes, in a pronated grip.\nInhale before unracking the bar, position it over your chest, lower it with your elbows away from your torso until it lightly touches your mid-chest, then press back up.",
  "Press inclinado en multipower": "Lying on an incline bench in the Smith machine, with your head, back, and feet firmly supported, grip the bar symmetrically slightly wider than shoulder-width, directly above your eyes, in a pronated grip.\nInhale before unracking the bar, position it over your chest, lower it with your elbows away from your torso until it lightly touches your upper chest, then press back up.",
  "Press declinado en multipower": "Lying on a decline bench in the Smith machine, with your head and back firmly supported and feet secured, grip the bar symmetrically slightly wider than shoulder-width, directly above your eyes, in a pronated grip.\nInhale before unracking the bar, position it over your chest, lower it with your elbows away from your torso until it lightly touches your lower chest, then press back up.",
  "Sentadilla sumo multipower": "To perform this exercise, stand with your legs apart and your feet pointed slightly outward.\nThen grip the bar in a pronated grip and rest it behind your head, on your traps and rear delts.\nNext, bend your knees until your thighs are parallel to the floor.\nThroughout the exercise, keep your abs and lower back firmly braced.\nMake sure not to lift your heels.",
  "Sentadilla multipower": "To perform this exercise, stand with your feet turned slightly outward and set a bit wider than hip-width.\nRest the bar behind your head, across your traps and rear delts.\nBend your knees until your thighs are roughly parallel to the floor.\nKeep your abs and lower back firmly braced, and avoid lifting your heels.\nIf needed, you can place a small wedge under your heels.",
  "Sentadillla bulgara multipower": "Standing, holding the bar in a pronated grip across your traps and rear delts as in the Smith machine squat, bend one knee to rest the top of that foot on a bench behind you — the leg on the floor takes almost all the weight as you descend — and lower your weight onto the front leg, bringing the rear knee toward the floor.\nYour back should stay straight and your front foot should stay aligned under your knee.\nFinally, drive back up by pushing through the front leg.",
  "Zancadas en multipower": "Standing, holding the bar in a pronated grip across your traps and rear delts as in the Smith machine squat, step one leg back and lower your weight onto the front leg, bringing the rear knee toward the floor.\nYour back should stay straight and your front foot should stay aligned under your knee.\nFinally, drive back up by pushing through the front leg.",
  "Remo en multipower": "Standing, hinge your torso forward while keeping it straight, leaning slightly forward with your knees slightly bent.\nHold the bar in a pronated grip with your hands set slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nThen pull with your elbows out until the bar reaches your abdomen.",
  "Press militar trasnuca multipower": "Sitting on a flat bench, hold the Smith machine bar in a pronated grip with your hands set slightly wider than shoulder-width; tilt your head slightly forward to lower the bar behind it, then press it back up until your arms are almost fully extended.\nThroughout the movement, your arms should stay parallel to your body and move close to your torso on the way down.",
  "Press militar multipower": "Sitting on a slightly inclined bench, hold the Smith machine bar in a pronated grip with your hands set slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nLower the bar to your upper chest/collarbone area, then press it back up until your arms are almost fully extended.\nThroughout the movement, your arms should stay parallel to your body and move close to your torso on the way down.",
  "Remo al menton multipower": "Standing in front of the Smith machine, with your legs slightly apart, keep your torso straight and braced.\nHold the bar in a pronated grip in front of your thighs, hands set slightly narrower than shoulder-width.\nThen pull the bar up toward your chin, keeping it close to your body throughout.\nDuring the movement, your elbows should rise and stay higher than your hands.",
  "Kaz press": "Lying on a bench, hold the Smith machine bar directly above your chest in a pronated grip, with your hands set slightly narrower than shoulder-width.\nLower it to chin height, then press it back up vertically.",
  "Press inclinado con mancuernas": "To perform this exercise, lie back on a bench set to around a 45° incline and place your feet on the floor while your back and head rest against the pad.\nWith a dumbbell in each hand, bring them up from your knees and hold them in a pronated grip directly above your forehead.\nYour forearms should move perpendicular to the floor, with a grip slightly wider than shoulder-width.",
  "Press declinado con mancuernas": "Lying on a decline bench, place your feet in the foot pads and rest your back and head on the pad; start the dumbbells from your knees.\nThen hold them directly above your forehead, in a pronated grip.\nYour forearms should move perpendicular to the floor, with a grip slightly wider than shoulder-width.",
  "Press mancuernas banco plano": "To perform this exercise, lie face up on a flat bench with your back and head supported and your feet on the floor.\nHold the dumbbells just above your chest, keeping them slightly apart.\nAs you lower the dumbbells, your forearms should move at a right angle to the floor.\nInhale as you start lowering the dumbbells to chest height, then exhale as you press them back to the starting position.\nMake sure to keep your elbows perpendicular to your body at all times.",
  "Aperturas con mancuernas": "This exercise is performed lying on a flat bench with your feet on the floor and your back and head supported.\nHold the dumbbells with your palms facing each other and elbows slightly bent, held slightly apart above your chest.\nInhale deeply as you lower the dumbbells to chest height without changing your elbow bend, then exhale as you raise them back to the start, keeping them slightly apart without touching.\nYour elbows move outward as if you were hugging something.",
  "Aperturas con mancuernas banco inclinado": "This exercise is performed lying on an incline bench with your feet on the floor and your back and head supported.\nHold the dumbbells with your palms facing each other and elbows slightly bent, held slightly apart above your chest.\nInhale deeply as you lower the dumbbells to chest height without changing your elbow bend, then exhale as you raise them back to the start, keeping them slightly apart without touching.\nYour elbows move outward as if you were hugging something.",
  "Puente de glúteo": "This exercise involves lying on the floor with your knees bent and your feet firmly planted on the floor.\nThen lift your hips to rest your weight on your feet and shoulders.",
  "Sentadilla búlgara": "Standing, holding a dumbbell, bend one knee to rest the top of that foot on a bench behind you — the leg on the floor takes almost all the weight as you descend — and lower your weight onto the front leg, bringing the rear knee toward the floor.\nYour back should stay straight and your front foot should stay aligned under your knee.\nFinally, drive back up by pushing through the front leg.",
  "Zancadas": "To perform this exercise, stand holding dumbbells or a bar in a pronated grip across your traps and rear delts.\nThen take a long step forward and lower your weight onto the front leg while your rear leg bends and its knee approaches the floor.\nKeep your back straight and stay balanced, making sure your front foot stays aligned under your knee.\nThen push off the front leg to drive back to the starting position.",
  "Puente de glúteo unilateral": "This exercise involves lying on the floor with your knees bent and your feet firmly planted on the floor.\nThen fully extend one leg and perform the lift using only the other leg.",
  "Hip thrust unilateral": "Sit on the floor with your knees bent and rest your upper back (shoulder blades) against a bench.\nWith one leg, extend your hip, aligning your back, glutes, and thigh at a 90-degree angle, while keeping the other leg bent or extended without resting it on the floor.\nAt the top, squeeze your glutes and hold for a few seconds.\nThen slowly lower your hips back to the starting position.",
  "Peso muerto rumano mancuernas": "Stand with your feet nearly straight and slightly apart, facing forward, holding the dumbbells against your thighs.\nHinge your torso forward without letting the dumbbells drift too far from your body, bending at the hips without arching your back.\nAs you lower, you should feel the pressure in your heels, not your toes.\nFocus on your hamstrings as you lower.",
  "Peso muerto rumano unilateral": "Standing, shift all your weight onto one leg while the other stays slightly back for balance; facing forward, hold the dumbbell in the hand on the same side as the supporting leg.\nHinge your torso forward without letting the dumbbell drift too far from your body, bending at the hips without arching your back.\nAs you lower, you should feel the pressure in your heels, not your toes.\nFocus on your hamstrings as you lower.",
  "HiperExtensiónes glúteo": "This exercise uses a hyperextension bench set at around a 45-degree angle.\nLie on the bench with your ankles secured under the pads and your hips resting on the pad.\nThen bend forward from nearly 90 degrees at the torso until you reach a straight line.\nAfter a brief pause, lower back down.",
  "Goblet squat": "Start standing with your feet shoulder-width apart, holding a weight (like a dumbbell or kettlebell) close to your chest with both hands, elbows tucked in.\nKeep your head up, shoulders back, and chest lifted.\nSlowly lower your body by bending your knees and hips, keeping your heels planted and your glutes pushing back.\nKeep the weight close to your chest.\nContinue lowering until your thighs are parallel to the floor or as low as you can go without losing good form, pause briefly, then drive through your heels to return to the starting position, keeping the weight close to your chest throughout.",
  "Curl supino antebrazo mancuerna": "Perform this seated or kneeling with your forearms resting on a bench.\nHold a dumbbell in a supinated grip and curl your fingers and wrist to lift it.",
  "Curl prono antebrazo mancuerna": "Perform this seated or kneeling with your forearms resting on a bench.\nHold a dumbbell in a pronated grip and curl your wrist to lift it.",
  "Sostener mancuernas": "This exercise focuses on improving grip strength and is performed by holding dumbbells for a short period of time.\nIt's important to grip the dumbbells firmly and hold them for the desired time, maintaining good posture and avoiding letting your shoulders round forward.",
  "Seal row banco inclinado mancuernas": "Set a bench to about a 45-degree angle.\nSit on the bench with your legs apart and your feet firmly planted on the floor.\nKeep your back straight.\nHold a dumbbell in each hand and extend your arms forward so they're parallel to the floor with the dumbbells facing each other.\nSlowly pull the dumbbells back while keeping your elbows close to your body.\nMake sure to maintain your back and elbow position throughout the movement.\nPull the dumbbells back until your elbows reach the level of your back.\nHold the position for a moment, then return the dumbbells to the starting position under control.",
  "Remo con mancuerna unilateral": "This exercise involves positioning yourself over a bench, supporting yourself with one hand, while the other hand holds a weight in a neutral position.\nThe opposite leg is placed diagonally and slightly back to keep your body aligned and horizontal.\nFrom the lowest position that allows a full lat stretch, pull the dumbbell toward your waist while moving your elbow close to your torso, keeping your forearm perpendicular to the floor.",
  "Bend over row": "To perform this exercise, stand upright with your hips bent at about a 45-degree angle, and your knees also slightly bent.\nOnce in this position, hold the dumbbells with your hands in a neutral position.\nThen pull with your elbows out to raise the dumbbells up to your abdomen.\nRemember to keep your posture firm and steady throughout the movement.",
  "Remo con mancuernas apoyado en banco inclinado": "To perform this exercise, rest your chest against an incline bench with your hips bent at about a 45-degree angle, and your knees also slightly bent.\nOnce in this position, hold the dumbbells with your hands in a neutral position.\nThen pull with your elbows out to raise the dumbbells up to your abdomen.\nRemember to keep your posture firm and steady throughout the movement.",
  "Pull over mancuerna": "Lying on your back on a bench with your head resting at the edge, hold a dumbbell vertically with both hands, palms in contact with the inner side of the top plate.\nWith your elbows slightly bent, slowly lower the dumbbell above and behind your head as you inhale deeply, keeping your elbow bend constant.\nThen return to the starting position while contracting your lats.",
  "Encogimientos": "This exercise is performed standing, with your legs slightly apart and your torso upright and braced.\nHold the dumbbells in a neutral grip at your sides.\nThen shrug your shoulders as high as possible and hold that position for a few seconds.\nThroughout this exercise, your arms should act as simple hooks holding the dumbbells while staying tense at all times.",
  "Press militar mancuerna": "In this exercise, sit on a slightly inclined or upright bench and hold the dumbbells in a pronated grip beside your head.\nThen press your arms up almost fully overhead, bringing them slightly together to maintain balance.\nThen slowly lower the dumbbells to ear height, keeping your arms parallel to your body and drawing them in toward your sides on the way down.\nIt's important to control the movement at all times to avoid injury and get the best results.",
  "Elevaciones laterales con mancuernas": "In this exercise, stand with your legs slightly bent and apart, facing forward.\nHold the dumbbells with your elbows slightly bent at your sides in a neutral position.\nThen raise your arms in abduction until they form a cross, bringing your elbows up to the same height as your hands and shoulders.\nYour palms should face down at the top of the movement.\nThen lower under control to avoid injury.\nIt's important to keep your elbows at the same height as your hands and shoulders throughout the exercise for the best results.",
  "Elevaciones frontales con mancuernas": "This exercise is performed standing, with your legs slightly apart and your torso braced.\nHold the dumbbells in a pronated grip in front of your thighs.\nThen raise the dumbbells forward to head height, preferably alternating sides, without changing your elbow bend and without bringing them toward the center of your body.\nIt's important to maintain proper posture and avoid arching your back during the movement.",
  "Pájaros con mancuernas banco inclinado": "In this exercise, lie face down on a lightly inclined bench to avoid swinging and strain on your lower back.\nHold the dumbbells in a neutral grip with your elbows pointing back and hanging in front of your body.\nThen raise the dumbbells out to the sides without changing your elbow bend, up to torso height.\nIt's important to keep your elbows far enough from your torso to avoid over-involving the lats.\nInhale as you begin to raise them and exhale as you lower, without fully releasing your breath.",
  "Elevacion lateral banco inclinado": "In this exercise, lie sideways on a bench set at about a 45-60 degree incline.\nHold a dumbbell with your elbow in a neutral position and raise your arm in abduction, bringing your elbow up to the same height as your hand and shoulder.\nKeep your palm facing down at the top of the movement.\nThen lower under control to avoid injury.\nThroughout the exercise, it's essential to keep your elbow at the same height as your hand and shoulder for the best results and to avoid shoulder injuries.\nThis exercise is ideal for strengthening the shoulder muscles and improving posture.",
  "Press francés mancuernas": "Lying face up with your feet on the floor, grip the dumbbells in a neutral position and extend your elbows above your eyes.\nThen lower your arms toward the sides of your head without moving your elbows from their original position.\nOnce you reach the bottom position, extend your arms back to the starting position.",
  "Extensión de tríceps overhead mancuerna": "This exercise can be performed standing or sitting with the support of a backrest that doesn't reach up to your head.\nThen brace your abs to hold your position and grip a dumbbell by the inside of the top plate.\nHold the dumbbell overhead with your elbows nearly fully extended and, without letting them flare out, lower the weight behind your head.",
  "Patada de tríceps mancuerna": "This exercise is performed by bracing one hand and knee on a flat bench, while the opposite hand holds a dumbbell in a neutral position.\nThe extended leg rests on the floor diagonally behind you, and your torso should stay horizontal and aligned.\nFrom an elbow bend of about 90 degrees, raise the dumbbell until your triceps are nearly at full extension.\nKeep your elbow close to your torso and don't let it move out of position during the exercise.",
  "Curl bíceps mancuernas": "This exercise can be done standing or sitting, with your legs slightly apart for balance.\nHold the dumbbells at your sides and brace your torso.\nAs you curl up under control, rotate your forearm so your palm faces your shoulder.",
  "Curl en banco scott mancuerna unilateral": "This exercise is performed seated, resting your triceps on a padded incline bench with your armpit at the edge.\nHold the dumbbell in a supinated grip with your elbow bent.\nLet the weight lower to a few degrees short of full extension, to avoid straining the elbow joint, then curl back up to the starting position.",
  "Curl de bíceps en banco inclinado": "This exercise is performed on a bench with the backrest set to about a 45-degree incline.\nHold the dumbbells at your sides, brace your torso, and let your arms hang down vertically.\nCurl up under control.",
  "Curl martillo": "This exercise can be done standing or sitting, with your legs slightly apart for balance.\nHold the dumbbells in a neutral grip at your sides and brace your torso.\nCurl up under control.",
  "Curl concentrado": "This exercise is performed seated on a bench, bending your torso slightly forward to rest one hand on your knee.\nYour other hand holds a dumbbell in a supinated grip, with your elbow braced against the inner side of your thigh.\nLower the weight until nearly fully extended, then curl it back up in a strict, controlled motion.",
  "Curl araña con mancuernas": "This exercise starts by inclining the bench's backrest.\nThen lean over it with your torso and abs pressed against the pad and your feet planted on the floor for good stability.\nHold the dumbbells with your arms in their natural position, hanging straight down.\nFrom this position, brace your core and glutes to maintain a strong, stable posture.\nAlso pull your shoulders back and engage your lats to protect your shoulders and maintain good posture.\nHold the dumbbells with a supinated grip and squeeze your biceps at the top, holding the contraction for 1-2 seconds.",
  "Press Banca": "To perform this exercise, lie on a flat bench with your head, back, and feet fully supported.\nThe bar should be held symmetrically with a grip slightly wider than shoulder-width, positioned over your eyeline in a pronated grip.\nTake a deep breath before unracking the bar.\nThen lower the bar with your elbows away from your body until it lightly touches your mid-chest.\nFinally, press the bar straight back up to its starting position.",
  "Press larsen": "To perform this exercise, lie on a flat bench with your head and back fully supported and your legs straight out, feet off the floor.\nThe bar should be held symmetrically with a grip slightly wider than shoulder-width, positioned over your eyeline in a pronated grip.\nTake a deep breath before unracking the bar.\nThen lower the bar with your elbows away from your body until it lightly touches your mid-chest.\nFinally, press the bar straight back up to its starting position.",
  "Press inclinado": "Lying on an incline bench, with your head, back, and feet firmly supported, grip the bar symmetrically slightly wider than shoulder-width, directly above your eyes, in a pronated grip.\nInhale before unracking the bar, position it over your chest, lower it with your elbows away from your torso until it lightly touches your upper chest, then press back up.",
  "Press declinado": "Lying on a decline bench, with your head and back firmly supported and feet secured, grip the bar symmetrically slightly wider than shoulder-width, directly above your eyes, in a pronated grip.\nInhale before unracking the bar, position it over your chest, lower it with your elbows away from your torso until it lightly touches your lower chest, then press back up.",
  "Svend press banco inclinado": "Sit on the bench with your feet planted on the floor and hold two dumbbells.\nPress them together and raise them close to your chest with your arms extended out in front.\nSqueeze your chest, then slowly lower the dumbbells and repeat.",
  "Press landmine": "To perform this exercise, set up a barbell at a 45-degree angle in a landmine attachment.\nHold the bar with one hand and raise it to chest height with your elbow bent.\nThen extend your arm outward to press the bar away from your body.\nKeep your core braced and your shoulders down.\nLower the bar under control back to your chest and repeat the movement several times.\nSwitch hands and repeat the exercise.",
  "Sentadilla barra alta": "To perform this exercise, stand with your feet turned slightly outward and set a bit wider than hip-width.\nHold a bar in a pronated grip behind your head, across your traps and rear delts.\nBend your knees, tracking toward your feet, until your thighs are parallel to the floor.\nKeep your abs and lower back firmly braced, and avoid lifting your heels.\nIf needed, you can place a small wedge under your heels.",
  "Sentadilla Frontal": "To perform this exercise, stand with your feet turned slightly outward and set a bit wider than hip-width.\nRest the bar on your front delts with your forearms crossed or not across your chest, chest wide and elbows raised.\nBend your knees, tracking toward your feet, until your thighs are parallel to the floor.\nKeep your abs and lower back firmly braced, and avoid lifting your heels.",
  "Sentadilla sumo": "To perform this exercise, stand with your feet turned slightly outward, opening your legs further as you descend.\nHold a bar in a pronated grip behind your head, across your traps and rear delts.\nBend your knees, tracking toward your feet, until your thighs are parallel to the floor.\nKeep your abs and lower back firmly braced, and avoid lifting your heels.",
  "Press militar barra": "In this exercise, sit on a slightly inclined bench or stand, and hold the bar in a pronated grip at head height.\nThen press your arms up almost fully overhead.\nThen slowly lower the bar to ear height, keeping your arms parallel to your body and drawing them in toward your sides on the way down.\nIt's important to control the movement at all times to avoid injury and get the best results.",
  "Remo al mentón": "Stand with your feet shoulder-width apart, holding a bar with a wide pronated grip.\nFrom this position, raise the bar up to chin height, keeping your elbows out and the bar close to your body.\nThen slowly lower the bar back to the starting position.",
  "Press militar trasnuca": "In this exercise, sit on a slightly inclined bench or stand, and hold the bar in a pronated grip at head height.\nThen press your arms up almost fully overhead.\nThen slowly lower the bar to ear height, behind your head, keeping your arms parallel to your body.\nIt's important to control the movement at all times to avoid injury and get the best results.",
  "Svend press banco plano": "Sit on the flat bench with your feet planted on the floor and hold two dumbbells.\nPress them together and raise them close to your chest with your arms extended out in front.\nSqueeze your chest, then slowly lower the dumbbells and repeat.",
  "Press francés barra Z": "This exercise is performed lying face up, with your feet on the floor.\nHold a short bar with your palms facing down, directly above your eyes, keeping your elbows straight.\nThen lower the bar toward your forehead, bending your elbows without moving them from their starting position.\nThen extend your elbows to raise the bar again, stopping a few degrees short of full extension to keep muscle tension throughout.",
  "Dead french press": "This exercise is performed lying on the floor face up.\nHold a short bar with your palms facing down, directly above your eyes, keeping your elbows straight.\nThen lower the bar toward your forehead, bending your elbows without moving them from their starting position, until it rests on the floor.\nThen extend your elbows to raise the bar again, stopping a few degrees short of full extension to keep muscle tension throughout.",
  "Peso muerto": "Stand in front of the bar with your feet shoulder-width apart and your knees slightly bent.\nThen hinge at the hips and lean your torso forward, keeping your back straight, and grip the bar on either side with a pronated or mixed grip.\nThen lift the bar off the floor by extending your legs and back until you're standing upright with the bar in your hands.\nFinally, lower the bar back to the floor under control, repeating the full movement.",
  "Peso muerto rumano": "Stand with your feet nearly straight and slightly apart, facing forward, holding the bar against your thighs.\nHinge your torso forward without letting the bar drift too far from your body, bending at the hips without arching your back.\nAs you lower, you should feel the pressure in your heels, not your toes.\nFocus on your hamstrings as you lower.",
  "Peso muerto sumo": "The sumo deadlift is a deadlift variant where you stand with your feet wider apart than usual, typically wider than shoulder-width, with your toes pointed outward.\nTo perform this exercise, grip the bar with your hands set narrower than shoulder-width, lean your torso forward, and lift the bar off the floor by extending your legs while keeping your back straight.\nLower the bar back to the floor under control, always keeping tension in your lower back, glutes, and legs.",
  "Peso muerto piernas rígidas": "To perform stiff-leg deadlifts, place a bar on the floor and stand behind it with your feet shoulder-width apart.\nWithout bending your knees, keep your back straight and your shoulders pulled back.\nGrip the bar with a pronated grip, hands set slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nLift the bar by extending your legs until fully straight, then lower it back to the floor under control.\nKeep your back straight throughout the exercise.",
  "Curl supino antebrazo barra": "Perform this seated or kneeling with your forearms resting on a bench.\nHold a bar in a supinated grip and curl your fingers and wrist to lift it.",
  "Curl prono antebrazo barra": "Perform this seated or kneeling with your forearms resting on a bench.\nHold a bar in a pronated grip and curl your fingers and wrist to lift it.",
  "Remo pendlay": "Place a bar on the floor and stand behind it with your feet shoulder-width apart.\nBend forward and grip the bar with a pronated grip, hands set slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nLift the bar off the floor and pull it toward your torso, keeping your elbows pointing up and back.\nLower the bar back to the floor under control and repeat the movement.\nKeep your back straight throughout the exercise.",
  "Remo 90": "Standing with your torso straight, hips bent about 90 degrees and knees slightly bent, everything braced, grip the bar with your hands set slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nPull with your elbows always out until the bar reaches your abdomen.",
  "Remo 45": "Standing with your torso straight, hips bent about 45 degrees and knees slightly bent, everything braced, grip the bar with your hands set slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nPull with your elbows always out until the bar reaches your abdomen.",
  "Remo 45 agarre supino": "Standing with your torso straight, hips bent about 45 degrees and knees slightly bent, everything braced, grip the bar in a supinated grip with your hands set slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nPull with your elbows always out until the bar reaches your abdomen.",
  "Curl bíceps barra": "In this exercise, start standing with your legs slightly apart for balance.\nYour torso, shoulders, and wrists should stay braced while you hold the bar in front of your thighs in a supinated grip, slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nCurl the bar up under control until you reach maximum flexion.",
  "Curl bíceps barra z": "In this exercise, start standing with your legs slightly apart for balance.\nYour torso, shoulders, and wrists should stay braced while you hold the EZ bar in front of your thighs in a supinated grip, slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nCurl the bar up under control until you reach maximum flexion.",
  "Curl bíceps barra z agarre prono": "In this exercise, start standing with your legs slightly apart for balance.\nYour torso, shoulders, and wrists should stay braced while you hold the EZ bar in front of your thighs in a pronated grip, slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nCurl the bar up under control until you reach maximum flexion.",
  "Curl en banco scott barra z": "This exercise is performed seated on a padded incline bench, resting your triceps on the pad with your armpits at the edge.\nHold the EZ bar in a supinated grip and bend your elbows.\nLower the bar to a few degrees short of full extension, to avoid straining the elbow joints, then curl back up to the starting position.",
  "Dead curl": "Standing with your legs slightly apart, torso, shoulders, and wrists braced, grip the bar resting on the pins of a rack in front of your thighs, in a supinated grip slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nCurl up under control to maximum flexion, then return under control until the bar rests back on the pins.",
  "Curl araña con barra": "This exercise starts by inclining the bench's backrest.\nThen lean over it with your torso and abs pressed against the pad and your feet planted on the floor for good stability.\nHold the bar with your arms in their natural position, hanging straight down.\nFrom this position, brace your core and glutes to maintain a strong, stable posture.\nAlso pull your shoulders back and engage your lats to protect your shoulders and maintain good posture.\nHold the bar with a supinated grip and squeeze your biceps at the top, holding the contraction for 1-2 seconds.",
  "Flexiones": "To perform this exercise, start in a plank position with your hands set slightly wider than shoulder-width and your toes supporting your body weight.\nLower your body toward the floor by bending your elbows, keeping your back straight and your elbows close to your body.\nPush back up to the starting position, keeping your abs and glutes braced.",
  "Flexiones pies elevados": "To perform this exercise, start in a plank position with your feet resting on a bench, hands set slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nLower your body toward the floor by bending your elbows, keeping your back straight and your elbows close to your body.\nPush back up to the starting position, keeping your abs and glutes braced.",
  "Flexiones rodillas apoyadas": "To perform this exercise, start in a plank position with your knees on the floor instead of your feet, hands set slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nLower your body toward the floor by bending your elbows, keeping your back straight and your elbows close to your body.\nPush back up to the starting position, keeping your abs and glutes braced.",
  "Fondos en paralelas": "Gripping the parallel bars with a neutral grip, torso upright and knees bent behind you, inhale and lower straight down, leaning your torso slightly forward.\nThe feeling should be one of pushing.\nTo do that, let your elbows flare out slightly from your torso.",
  "Curl nordico": "To perform this exercise, start kneeling on the floor with your ankles secured under a solid support, such as a bench or wall bars.\nThen lower your body toward the floor while keeping your hips in a neutral position, using your hamstrings to control the movement.\nPush back up to return to the starting position, using your glutes and leg muscles to drive yourself back up.",
  "Aguantar colgado en barra": "This exercise is as simple as doing sets of holding on to a bar for a set amount of time.\nYou can progress by increasing the thickness of the bar.",
  "Dominadas": "Hanging from a horizontal bar, grip it with a pronated grip, hands set wider than shoulder-width.\nUse your lats to pull and bring your upper chest toward the bar while arching your torso slightly back.\nYour legs can hang relaxed or cross behind you, knees bent or straight.",
  "Dominadas agarre neutro": "Hanging from a horizontal bar, grip it with a supinated grip.\nUse your lats to pull and bring your upper chest toward the bar while arching your torso slightly back.\nYour legs can hang relaxed or cross behind you, knees bent or straight.",
  "Dominadas agarre supino": "Hanging from a bar with the corresponding grip attachment, hold it with a neutral grip.\nUse your lats to pull and bring your upper chest toward the bar while arching your torso slightly back.\nYour legs can hang relaxed or cross behind you, knees bent or straight.",
  "Remo con peso corporal": "Set the bar at a low height and rest your heels on a bench or the floor, keeping your body nearly horizontal.\nPerform a movement that's a cross between a pull-up and a barbell row, pulling up to a midpoint between the two and bringing your chest toward the bar.",
  "Remo en trx": "Set the TRX at a low height and rest your heels on a bench or the floor, keeping your body nearly horizontal.\nPerform a movement that's a cross between a pull-up and a barbell row, pulling up to a midpoint between the two and bringing your chest toward the handles.",
  "HiperExtensión lumbar": "To perform this exercise, start lying face down on a hyperextension bench with your ankles secured under the padded supports and your hands crossed behind your head.\nThen raise your torso upward using your lower back muscles, keeping your head in line with your spine and your elbows out to the sides.\nLower your torso back down to the starting position, staying in control throughout.",
  "Extensión lumbar en el suelo": "To perform this exercise, start lying face down on the floor with your arms extended forward and your legs extended back.\nThen raise your torso upward using your lower back muscles, keeping your head in line with your spine and your elbows out to the sides.\nLower your torso back down to the starting position, staying in control throughout.",
  "Fondos paralelas tríceps": "Gripping the parallel bars with a narrow neutral grip, torso upright and knees bent behind you, inhale and lower straight down, keeping your torso nearly upright to avoid involving the chest too much.\nThe feeling should be one of extending your arms — to do that, never let your elbows flare far from your torso.",
  "Fondos tríceps en banco": "Supporting yourself on a bench, torso upright and feet elevated or on the floor, inhale and lower straight down.\nThe feeling should be one of extending your arms.",
  "Crunch abdominal": "To perform this exercise, start lying face up on the floor with your knees bent and feet flat on the floor.\nThen lift your head and shoulders up using your abs, keeping your hands behind your head or crossed over your chest.\nLower your torso back down to the starting position, staying in control throughout.",
  "Plancha": "To perform this exercise, start lying face down on the floor with your forearms on the floor and your elbows aligned under your shoulders.\nThen lift your body off the floor, keeping a straight line from shoulders to feet, and hold the position for a set amount of time.\nLower your body back down to the starting position, staying in control throughout.",
  "Rueda abdominal": "To perform this exercise, start kneeling on the floor with your hands gripping an ab wheel on the floor in front of your body.\nThen roll the wheel forward as you extend your arms and lower your torso toward the floor, keeping your back straight and your abs braced.\nPull the wheel back while contracting your abs to return to the starting position.",
  "Hip thrust": "Sit on the floor with your knees bent and rest your upper back (shoulder blades) against a bench.\nPosition the bar (with padding to avoid discomfort) at hip height, and extend your hips, aligning your back, glutes, and thighs at a 90-degree angle.\nAt the top, squeeze your glutes and hold for a few seconds.\nThen slowly lower your hips back to the starting position.",
  "Crunch invertido": "To perform this exercise, start lying face up on a bench, holding onto the ends of it with your legs raised in the air.\nThen lift your hips off the surface, keeping your knees vertical, while contracting your abs.\nThen lower back to the starting position, staying in control throughout.",
  "Crunch oblicuos": "To perform this exercise, start lying on your side on the floor with your knees bent and your hands behind your head.\nThen lift your torso up while contracting your oblique muscles.\nThen lower your torso back to the starting position, staying in control throughout.\nComplete all reps on the same side before switching sides.",
  "Plancha lateral oblicuos": "To perform this exercise, start lying on your side on the floor with your forearm on the floor and your elbow under your shoulder.\nThen lift your body off the floor, keeping your back straight and your abs braced.\nHold this position for a few seconds before lowering your body to the floor, and complete all reps on the same side before switching sides.",
  "Abdominales bicicleta": "To perform this exercise, start lying face up on the floor with your hands behind your head and your legs raised in the air at a 90-degree angle.\nThen bring your right elbow toward your left knee while extending your right leg out in the air.\nThen switch sides, bringing your left elbow toward your right knee while extending your left leg out in the air.\nRepeat this movement in a pedaling pattern, as if you were riding a bicycle.",
  "Plancha lateral aductor": "To perform this exercise, start lying on your side on the floor with your forearm on the floor, your elbow under your shoulder, and one leg resting on a bench.\nThen lift your body off the floor, supporting your weight on the raised leg and your forearm, keeping your back straight and your abs braced.\nHold this position for a few seconds before lowering your body to the floor.",
  "Gemelos de pie con barra": "To perform this exercise, start standing with your feet shoulder-width apart and place a loaded barbell across your upper back, holding it with your hands behind your neck, or use a dedicated calf-raise machine.\nThen raise your heels and rise up onto your toes, holding the position for a few seconds before slowly lowering your heels back to the floor.\nRepeat this movement for several reps to work your calf muscles.",
  "Cinta": "This machine has a moving running surface driven by a belt, powered by a motor.\nTo use it, step onto the treadmill and adjust the speed and incline of the running surface to simulate different intensity levels of walking or running.",
  "Correr": "Running is a form of cardiovascular exercise, an individual or group activity, and it can be adapted to different skill levels and training goals.\nIt's important to run with proper technique to avoid injury, and make sure to warm up before starting the activity.",
  "Bici estatica": "This machine has a seat, handlebars, and pedals, and you can adjust the pedaling resistance to increase or decrease the training intensity.\nThe stationary bike is an excellent option for those who want to do cardio exercise indoors, since it's low-impact and easy to use.",
  "Bici": "Cycling is a low-impact physical activity that can be good for your health, helping improve cardiovascular endurance, muscular strength, and coordination.\nCycling can also be an enjoyable way to exercise and get outdoors.",
  "AirBike": "The air bike combines pedaling with a back-and-forth arm movement, resulting in a high-intensity, full-body workout.\nThe faster you pedal, the more air resistance is generated, making the workout more intense.",
  "Trotar": "Jogging is a form of cardiovascular exercise, an individual or group activity, and it can be adapted to different skill levels and training goals.\nIt's important to run with proper technique to avoid injury, and make sure to warm up before starting the activity.",
  "Bicicleta elíptica": "The elliptical trainer has two pedals and two moving arms that move in an elliptical path, making the motion smooth and low-impact on your joints.\nUsing the elliptical, you can adjust the resistance to increase exercise intensity and work different muscle groups in the body, such as the glutes, legs, arms, and abs.",
  "Burpees": "The exercise starts standing, then you drop into a plank position with your hands on the floor, perform a push-up, bring your legs in toward your chest, and finally jump up with your arms overhead.\nBurpees are a high-intensity exercise that combines strength, endurance, and cardio movements.\nBurpees are popular because they're very effective for burning calories and improving overall fitness in a short amount of time.",
  "Jumping jacks": "To do jumping jacks, start standing with your arms at your sides and your legs together.\nThen jump while spreading your legs out to the sides and raising your arms overhead.\nThen jump again to return to the starting position with your legs together and arms at your sides.\nJumping jacks are an effective way to raise your heart rate and improve cardiovascular health.\nThey can also help improve coordination and mobility.\nThey're a low-impact exercise that can be done anywhere without any special equipment.",
  "Skipping o elevación de rodillas": "To do skipping, start standing with your feet shoulder-width apart and your arms at your sides.\nThen start running in place, alternately lifting your knees toward your chest while moving your arms in sync.\nThe goal is to maintain a fast, steady pace to raise your heart rate and burn calories.\nSkipping is a low-impact exercise that can be done anywhere without any special equipment.",
  "Saltar a la comba": "Jumping rope is a low-impact exercise that helps improve cardiovascular endurance, coordination, and agility.\nIt's also a fun way to exercise and can be done anywhere with a suitable rope.",
  "Golpear al saco": "Hitting a heavy bag is a high-intensity exercise that helps improve muscular strength and endurance, coordination, agility, and precision.\nIt's important to use proper technique to avoid injury and maximize the benefits of the exercise.",
  "Rope training": "To do this, you need one or more ropes, anchored at one end to a wall or frame and held in your hands at the other end.\nTo start, take a standing position, hold the ropes in your hands, and begin moving them in different patterns and rhythms, which requires significant muscular effort.\nThe movements can include waves, slams, circles, and more, and you can vary the duration, intensity, and number of reps.\nRope training is a high-intensity exercise that engages many muscles in the body, including the arms, shoulders, chest, back, and abs.\nIt also improves cardiovascular endurance and coordination, and can be used for both strength training and high-intensity training.\nIt's important to use proper technique to avoid injury and maximize the benefits of the exercise.",
  "Salto al cajón": "Box jumps mainly work the leg muscles, including the quads, glutes, and calves, but also engage your upper body — shoulders and arms — to help generate momentum during the jump.\nIt's important to use proper technique to avoid injury and make sure to perform the jump safely and effectively.",
  "Empujar cajón": "To perform this exercise, place a heavy sled/box on the floor and get into position behind it with your hands resting on top of it.\nThen push the box forward with your hands while walking backward, keeping your back straight.\nThe goal of the sled push is to strengthen your upper body — shoulders, arms, chest, and back — as well as your legs, which drive the push forward.\nThis exercise can be done in sets and reps to increase training intensity.",
  "Rowerg o máquina de Remo": "Sit on the seat that slides forward and back along a rail, and secure your feet in the foot straps.\nThen grip the machine's handle and perform a rowing motion, pulling the handle toward your body as you lean back, then extending your arms and legs to return to the starting position.",
  "Máquina de escaleras o stepper": "The stair machine, also known as a stair climber or stepper, is a cardio machine that simulates the motion of climbing stairs.\nYou step up and down on platforms that move up and down, while holding onto the machine's handrails to keep your balance and control your pace.\nLike running and other forms of cardio exercise, using the stair machine can improve cardiovascular health, burn calories, and reduce stress.",
  "Sentadillas con salto": "To perform this exercise, start in a squat position, with your feet shoulder-width apart and your knees bent at a 90-degree angle.\nFrom this position, jump explosively into the air, fully extending your legs and reaching your arms overhead.\nAs you land, return to the starting squat position and repeat the movement several times in a row.",
  "Mountain climbers": "To perform mountain climbers, start in a high plank position, with your hands and feet on the floor and your body straight.\nFrom this position, bring one foot forward and outside the hand on the same side, keeping the opposite leg extended back.\nThen return the foot back and repeat the movement with the other leg.",
  "Butt kicks": "To perform butt kicks, start standing with your feet shoulder-width apart.\nThen kick one leg back and up, bending your knee and bringing your heel toward your glute, then lower the leg back to the floor, alternating with the other leg.",
  "Desplazamientos laterales": "To perform lateral shuffles, start standing with your feet shoulder-width apart and place a cone or object to one side.\nThen shuffle sideways toward the cone with short, quick steps, maintaining an athletic stance with your head up.\nRepeat this movement several times in one direction, then switch directions.\nLateral shuffles are a cardio and agility exercise involving quick, short lateral movements to improve coordination, speed, and reaction ability.",
  "Botar pelota medicinal": "To perform this exercise, start standing with your feet shoulder-width apart and the medicine ball on the floor.\nThen squat down to pick up the ball and stand back up holding it in front of your body.\nThen throw the ball into the air forcefully, using mainly your upper body muscles, and catch it as it falls back down.\nThis movement can be repeated several times in a set.",
  "Skierg": "The SkiErg is a training machine that simulates the motion of cross-country skiing.\nIt has two paddle-shaped handles that move back and forth while you stand facing the machine.\nThe SkiErg provides an intense cardio workout and works your upper body muscles, especially your arms, shoulders, and back.\nYou can also perform strength and endurance work on the SkiErg by adjusting the machine's resistance.",
  "Step ups": "Step-ups are an exercise that involves stepping up and down off a bench or box with one foot at a time while maintaining balance and supporting your body weight.\nThis exercise is a good workout for your leg muscles, especially the quads, glutes, and calves.\nIt can also be a good cardio exercise, depending on the intensity and duration.",
  "Sissy squat": "This exercise is performed standing with your feet set at hip-width.\nOne hand holds onto a side support while the other rests on your waist or chest.\nIf you want to add weight, you can hold a plate.\nThe movement involves deeply bending your knees while leaning your torso slightly back, avoiding bending too much at the hips.\nDuring the descent, your heels can lift slightly off the floor.\nAs you rise, it's important not just to stand back up, but also to travel forward slightly.\nThe focus is on the quads to maximize effort and get the greatest benefit.",
  "Spoto press": "This is performed in a position similar to the bench press, but instead of lowering the bar to your chest, you stop it a few centimeters above your chest.\nThis pause helps keep tension on the muscles for longer and can also help correct your bench press technique.\nProper technique includes keeping your elbows close to your body, maintaining good posture, and controlling the bar at all times.",
  "Remo meadows": "This is performed at a T-bar row landmine setup with a loaded bar, held with one hand while your other arm rests on your knee.\nLean your torso forward and pull the bar up toward your chest, then slowly lower it back to the starting position.",
  "Belt squat": "The belt squat is a strength exercise in which the athlete wears a lifting belt and hangs suspended while performing squats.\nThe belt is connected to a weight platform that hangs below the athlete's body and provides resistance during the squat movement.\nThis exercise focuses on developing the legs, especially the quads, without loading the lower back.",
  "Buenos días con barra": "Good mornings with a barbell are a strength exercise that works the lower back and hamstrings.\nTo do it, place a bar across your upper back, hinge forward from your hips while keeping your legs straight and your back straight, then return to the starting position.\nThis exercise should be performed with proper technique to avoid injury, and it's important to keep your back straight throughout the movement.",
  "Buenos días en multipower": "The Smith machine good morning is a posterior-chain strength exercise performed on a Smith machine.\nThe exercise involves placing a bar across your shoulders and then hinging forward at the hips while keeping your knees slightly bent.\nYour upper body leans forward as your hips push back, stretching your hamstrings and lower back.\nThen contract your posterior chain muscles to return to the starting position.",
  "Curl 21": "The 21s curl is a biceps training exercise performed with a bar or dumbbells.\nIt's divided into three parts: the first is seven reps from the bottom position to the halfway point of the range of motion, the second is seven reps from the halfway point to the top, and the third is seven full-range reps.",
  "Curl de bíceps alterno": "To perform this exercise, you'll need a pair of dumbbells.\nStarting with the dumbbells at your sides, curl one dumbbell up toward your shoulder as you bend your elbow.\nLower the dumbbell back to the starting position, then repeat the same movement with the other arm.\nAlternate reps between each arm to complete the set.",
  "Curl de bíceps agarre prono polea": "To perform this exercise, you need a pulley set to a suitable height, with a straight bar or an EZ bar attached.\nStand facing the pulley, grip the bar in a pronated grip, and bend your elbows to bring the bar toward your shoulders, keeping your elbows tucked to your body.\nThen extend your elbows to lower the bar back to the starting position, avoiding losing tension in your biceps throughout the exercise.",
  "Curl de bíceps polea unilateral": "To perform this exercise, you need a pulley set to a suitable height, with a single handle attachment.\nStand facing the pulley, grip the handle in a supinated grip, and bend your elbow to bring the handle toward your shoulder, keeping your elbow tucked to your body.\nThen extend your elbow to lower back to the starting position, avoiding losing tension in your biceps throughout the exercise.",
  "Elevaciones laterales sentado": "To perform this exercise, sit on a bench with a dumbbell in each hand.\nWith your palms facing in, raise your arms out to the sides until they're parallel to the floor.\nHold the position for a moment, then slowly lower the dumbbells.\nIt's important to keep your elbows slightly bent throughout the movement to avoid shoulder injury.",
  "Máquina de dominadas asistidas": "This exercise is performed using a machine that has an adjustable knee pad and a bar to grip with your hands.\nPlace your knees on the pad and grip the bar with your hands, then pull your body up toward the bar using your upper body muscles.\nThe machine provides assistance during the movement, letting you perform the exercise with less effort and build technique and strength over time.",
  "Gemelo en prensa": "To perform this exercise, position yourself on the leg press platform with your toes on the lower edge of the platform and your heels hanging off.\nThen lower your heels to stretch your calf muscles and rise up onto your toes to contract them.\nRepeat this movement several times to work your calf muscles.",
  "Box squat": "To perform this exercise, stand with your feet turned slightly outward and set a bit wider than hip-width, with a plyo box positioned behind you.\nHold a bar behind your head, across your traps and rear delts.\nBend your knees until your glutes touch the box, then stand back up.\nKeep your abs and lower back firmly braced.",
  "Gemelo en multipower": "To perform this exercise, start standing under a bar in a Smith machine with a step block.\nPosition the bar across your upper back.\nThen raise your heels off the floor to lift the bar's weight, keeping your legs straight.\nSlowly lower your heels back to the floor and repeat the movement.\nThis exercise helps strengthen and tone your calf muscles.",
  "Gemelo/soleo en máquina sentado": "Position your knees under the machine's pads and your feet on a raised platform with your toes pointing forward.\nThen push up through the balls of your feet to raise your heels and contract your calf muscles, before slowly lowering back to the starting position.",
  "Gemelo en máquina de pie": "Position your shoulders under the machine's pads and your feet on a raised platform with your toes pointing forward.\nThen push up through the balls of your feet to raise your heels and contract your calf muscles, before slowly lowering back to the starting position.",
  "Glúteo medio con banda elástica": "To perform this exercise, place the band around your ankles and stand with your feet hip-width apart, knees slightly bent.\nFrom this position, lift one leg out to the side, keeping the band taut throughout.\nHold the leg up for one or two seconds, then lower slowly.",
  "Hombro posterior en máquina": "To perform this exercise, sit on the machine and adjust the seat and chest pad to the proper height.\nThen place your elbows on the machine's pads and drive your arms back.",
  "Reverse hyper en banco": "To start, rest your chest on the bench.\nThen extend your legs back so they're perpendicular to your body, and raise your legs, keeping them straight, up to hip height or slightly higher, keeping your glutes and lower back contracted throughout the movement.\nThen slowly lower back to the starting position and repeat the exercise.",
  "Pájaros con mancuernas": "To perform this exercise, stand with your legs slightly apart and slightly bent.\nKeep your torso braced and leaning forward until it's nearly horizontal.\nHold the dumbbells with a neutral grip and your elbows pointing back, holding them in front of your body.\nRaise the dumbbells out to the sides, keeping a slight bend in your elbows, bringing them up to torso height.\nMake sure to keep your elbows away from your torso to avoid over-involving your back muscles.",
  "Pájaros con mancuernas sentado": "To perform this exercise, sit at the edge of a bench.\nKeep your torso braced and leaning slightly forward.\nHold the dumbbells with a neutral grip and your elbows pointing back, holding them below your legs.\nRaise the dumbbells out to the sides, keeping a slight bend in your elbows, bringing them up to torso height.\nMake sure to keep your elbows away from your torso to avoid over-involving your back muscles.",
  "Patada de glúteo en multipower": "In a quadruped position, lift a bent knee slightly off the floor with your foot pressing against the bar, then kick upward.\nThe movement should be controlled, rising to just past horizontal, without arching your back too much.\nRest on your elbows instead of your hands if that makes it easier.\nDuring hip extension, you can also extend the leg to shift some of the work to the hamstrings.",
  "Peso muerto barra hexagonal": "Stand in the center of the bar, feet shoulder-width apart.\nFrom this position, lower the bar toward the floor while keeping your back straight and bending your knees and hips.\nOnce the bar reaches the floor, stand back up while keeping your back straight and driving up with your legs and glutes.",
  "Peso muerto en déficit de altura": "The deficit deadlift is a deadlift variant performed from an elevated platform or box, increasing the distance the bar has to travel.\nTo perform the exercise, place a platform under your feet, raising them off the floor.\nThen grip the bar with a pronated or mixed grip, keeping your back straight and shoulders pulled back.\nLift the bar in a controlled motion, bringing it up to a standing position.",
  "Peso muerto con parada": "To perform this exercise, start standing with your feet slightly apart and the bar resting on the floor in front of you.\nGrip the bar with a pronated or mixed grip and slowly lift it to knee height, maintaining good posture with your arms and back straight.\nOnce the bar reaches knee height, pause and hold that position briefly before finishing the lift to a full standing position.",
  "Peso muerto rumano piernas rígidas": "To perform stiff-leg Romanian deadlifts, place a bar on the floor and stand behind it with your feet shoulder-width apart.\nWithout bending your knees, keep your back straight and your shoulders pulled back.\nGrip the bar with a pronated grip, hands set slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nLift the bar by extending your legs until fully straight, then lower the bar under control toward the floor without letting it touch.",
  "Peso muerto rumano en multipower": "Stand with your feet nearly straight and slightly apart, facing forward, holding the Smith machine bar against your thighs.\nHinge your torso forward without letting the bar drift too far from your body, bending at the hips without arching your back.\nAs you lower, you should feel the pressure in your heels, not your toes.\nFocus on your hamstrings as you lower.",
  "Pin press": "To perform this exercise, lie on a flat bench with your head, back, and feet fully supported.\nThe bar should be held symmetrically with a grip slightly wider than shoulder-width, positioned over your eyeline in a pronated grip.\nTake a deep breath before unracking the bar.\nThen lower the bar with your elbows away from your body until it rests on the pins, holding it there for 1-2 seconds.\nFinally, press the bar straight back up to its starting position.",
  "Pin squat": "To perform this exercise, stand with your feet turned slightly outward and set a bit wider than hip-width.\nHold a bar in a pronated grip behind your head, across your traps and rear delts.\nBend your knees, tracking toward your feet, until the bar rests on the safety pins.\nKeep your abs and lower back firmly braced, and avoid lifting your heels.",
  "Prensa unilateral": "To perform this exercise, sit on the machine with your back and hips supported.\nPlace one foot on the platform at shoulder height and keep the other on the floor.\nThen lower under control until that thigh comes close to your torso without lifting your hips off the backrest.\nFinally, press back up in a controlled but explosive manner until you're nearly at full extension.",
  "Press arnold": "The movement starts by holding the dumbbells in a neutral position (palms facing each other) at shoulder height with your elbows bent.\nFrom that position, press the dumbbells up while rotating your palms forward until your arms are fully extended overhead.\nThen lower the dumbbells back to the starting position while rotating your palms back to neutral.",
  "Press banca agarre cerrado": "To perform this exercise, lie on a flat bench with your head, back, and feet fully supported.\nThe bar should be held symmetrically with a close grip, slightly narrower than shoulder-width, positioned over your eyeline in a pronated grip.\nTake a deep breath before unracking the bar.\nThen lower the bar with your elbows away from your body until it lightly touches your mid-chest.\nFinally, press the bar straight back up to its starting position.",
  "Press militar en máquina": "In this exercise, sit on the machine, adjust it to your height, and grip the handles.\nThen press your arms up almost fully overhead.\nThen slowly lower, keeping your arms parallel to your body and drawing them in toward your sides on the way down.\nIt's important to control the movement at all times to avoid injury and get the best results.",
  "Push press con barra": "To perform the push press, start standing with your feet shoulder-width apart, holding a bar at shoulder height.\nThen bend your knees and dip into a slight squat as you drive the bar upward with a powerful, explosive push from your legs and hips.\nAs the bar moves up, press with your arms to bring it overhead, making sure to fully extend your arms.\nLower the bar under control back to the starting position and repeat the movement for the desired number of reps.",
  "Push press con mancuernas": "The dumbbell push press is performed standing with your feet shoulder-width apart and the dumbbells held at shoulder height, elbows bent.\nFrom this starting position, do a slight knee bend and an explosive leg drive to help push the dumbbells upward.\nAt the same time, extend your arms and press the dumbbells overhead, keeping your elbows locked out at the top.\nOnce the dumbbells are fully overhead, slowly lower them back to the starting position and repeat the movement for the desired number of reps.",
  "Rack pull": "To start, set the bar on the rack at a height slightly above knee level.\nThen stand in front of the bar and grip it with your hands set slightly wider than shoulder-width.\nThen lift the bar off the rack and hold it briefly in a standing position.\nThen, keeping your back straight, lower the bar back to the starting position while keeping your legs and arms straight.",
  "Remo gironda agarre prono": "Sitting facing the V-bar row, with your knees slightly bent and firmly planted on the platforms, grip the handle in a pronated grip with your arms almost fully extended.\nThen pull the handle toward your stomach while expanding your chest.\nIt's important to keep the movement short in the lower back to avoid injury.",
  "Remo gironda agarre supino": "Sitting facing the V-bar row, with your knees slightly bent and firmly planted on the platforms, grip the handle in a supinated grip with your arms almost fully extended.\nThen pull the handle toward your stomach while expanding your chest.\nIt's important to keep the movement short in the lower back to avoid injury.",
  "Remo unilateral polea": "To perform this exercise, start by adjusting the pulley height to slightly below shoulder height.\nThen grip the handle with one hand, step back, and place the foot on the same side as the gripping hand forward.\nFrom this starting position, extend your arm forward, then pull it toward your torso, keeping your elbow close to your body and your back straight.\nLower back to the starting position and repeat the movement for the desired number of reps, before switching sides and performing the exercise with the other hand.",
  "Remo en polea baja": "Standing, bend your torso forward about 45 degrees and keep your knees slightly bent while gripping the bar attached to the low pulley in a pronated grip.\nThen pull the bar up, expanding your chest, until it reaches your upper abdomen.\nAlso, keep your back firm and straight throughout the exercise.",
  "Sentadilla barra baja": "To perform this exercise, stand with your feet turned slightly outward and set a bit wider than hip-width.\nHold a bar behind your head, in an area a bit lower than your mid-traps and rear delts.\nBend your knees until your thighs are roughly parallel to the floor.\nKeep your abs and lower back firmly braced, and avoid lifting your heels.",
  "Zancadas con barra": "To perform this exercise, stand holding the bar in a pronated grip across your traps and rear delts.\nThen take a long step forward and lower your weight onto the front leg while your rear leg bends and its knee approaches the floor.\nKeep your back straight and stay balanced, making sure your front foot stays aligned under your knee.\nThen push off the front leg to drive back to the starting position.",
  "Patada de gúteo bilateral en multipower": "Lying on the floor, lift your feet by pressing against the bar, then kick upward.\nThe movement should be controlled, rising to just past horizontal, without arching your back too much.\nDuring hip extension, you can also extend the legs to shift some of the work to the hamstrings.",
  "Prensa pendular": "To perform this exercise, lie back on the pendulum press with your back and hips supported.\nYour feet should be placed on the platform slightly wider than hip-width, with your toes turned out slightly.\nThen lower under control until your thighs come close to your torso without lifting your hips off the backrest.\nFinally, press back up in a controlled but explosive manner until you're nearly at full extension.",
  "Press tumbado en máquina": "Lie back on the machine.\nGrip the handles in a pronated position and set your hands slightly wider than shoulder width.\nAs you inhale, lower the handles to mid-chest height and then press them back up.\nKeep your elbows perpendicular to your body and don't lock them out at the top.\nExhale as you finish pressing up.",
  "Peso muerto rumano piernas rígidas mancuernas ": "Stand with your feet nearly straight and slightly apart, facing forward, holding the dumbbells against your thighs.\nHinge your torso forward without letting the dumbbells drift too far from your body, bending at the hips without arching your back.\nAs you lower, you should feel the pressure in your heels, not your toes.\nFocus on your hamstrings as you lower.",
  "Remo bajo en máquina": "To perform this exercise, sit with your chest and abdomen supported.\nGrip the handle and pull it toward your stomach while pushing your chest out to achieve a good lat contraction.\nA key advantage of this exercise is that it avoids strain on the lower back since the torso is supported.\nThe downside is that it can restrict breathing due to the compression on the abdomen and chest.",
  "Sentadilla V": "To perform this exercise, position yourself on the machine with your shoulders under the pads.\nThe quadriceps are the main muscles worked in this exercise, while the hamstrings play a secondary role and are engaged to a lesser degree than the other muscles mentioned.",
  "Sentadilla sissy en máquina": "This exercise is performed using a low bench for support to keep your balance.\nPlace your feet at hip-width and position the bench behind you.\nThe movement starts by bending your knees as your body leans back under control, bringing your hips toward the bench without losing torso alignment.\nLightly touch the bench with your glutes without fully sitting down, then return to the starting position by extending your legs.\nThis exercise targets the quads, helping to develop them to the fullest with an extended range of motion.",
  "Sentadilla con barra safety": "To perform this exercise, stand with your feet turned slightly outward and set a bit wider than hip-width.\nHold a bar behind your head, in an area a bit lower than your mid-traps and rear delts.\nBend your knees until your thighs are roughly parallel to the floor.\nKeep your abs and lower back firmly braced, and avoid lifting your heels.",
  "Seal row": "Lie face down on the bench.\nKeep your back straight.\nGrip the bar and pull it back while keeping your elbows close to your body.\nMake sure to maintain your back and elbow position throughout the movement.\nPull the bar back until your elbows reach the level of your back.\nHold the position for a moment, then return the bar to the starting position under control.",
  "Peso muerto rumano barra hexagonal": "Stand with your feet nearly straight and slightly apart, facing forward, and grip the bar.\nHinge your torso forward, bending at the hips, without arching your back.\nAs you lower, you should feel the pressure in your heels, not your toes.\nFocus on your hamstrings as you lower.",
  "Oblicuos en máquina": "This exercise is performed on a machine specifically designed to work the obliques.\nSit on the machine's seat with your back resting against the pad and adjust the position so your hips are aligned with the machine's axis of rotation.\nGrip the side handles or place your arms in the position indicated by the machine's design.\nThe movement involves rotating your torso to one side under control, contracting your oblique muscles, then slowly returning to center.\nRepeat the movement to the opposite side, always staying in control throughout the back-and-forth motion.\nMake sure to avoid jerky movements and keep an upright posture to maximize oblique activation and avoid unnecessary strain on your lower back.",
  "Remo en gironda unilateral": "Sit at the V-bar cable row, with your knees slightly bent and firmly planted on the platforms, and grip the handle with a neutral grip.\nThen extend your arm almost fully and pull the handle toward your stomach while pushing your chest out to achieve a proper contraction.\nAccompany the movement with a slight lean from the lower back.",
  "Extensión de tríceps en máquina": "In this exercise, sit down on the seat.\nGripping the handles with your elbows resting on the pad, extend your arms forward without changing your elbow position.",
  "Extensión de tríceps overhead mancuerna unilateral": "This exercise can be performed standing or sitting with the support of a backrest that doesn't reach up to your head.\nThen brace your abs to hold your position and grip a dumbbell by the inside of the top plate.\nHold the dumbbell overhead with your elbow nearly fully extended and, without letting it flare out, lower the weight behind your head.",
};
